## The problem

Your Task Manager grew. Forty routes now: tasks, categories, auth, attachments, comments, reminders, exports. `app.ts` is three hundred lines of `app.use(...)` in an order nobody dares touch, because someone once moved the auth middleware two lines up and uploads broke for a day.

Then two things land in the same week. A teammate adds `formatDueDate` to `utils/date.ts` — the second copy of a function that already lives in `services/task.service.ts`, with a different timezone bug. And you sit down to unit-test `listTasks` and can't, because the service file imports the Supabase client at the top. The instant Jest loads that module it wants real credentials and a real network. Your "unit test" boots the whole app, takes nine seconds, and fails on CI because there's no `.env`.

Neither of those is a bug you can point at in a stack trace. They are the bill for total freedom, arriving late.

## The idea

Look closely at why that service is untestable. It never *receives* the client — it *reaches out and takes one*:

```ts
// services/task.service.ts — the service constructs its own world
import { supabase } from "../config/supabase";

export async function listTasks(userId: string) {
  return supabase.from("tasks").select("*").eq("user_id", userId);
}
```

Dependency injection turns that around. A class **declares** what it needs, as constructor parameters, and something else decides what to hand it. That something else is the DI container. Two things fall out immediately: you can hand it a fake in a test, and you can hand it a different real thing in production without touching the file.

The second idea is modules, and you already do this on mobile. A well-factored React Native feature folder has its own navigator, its own hooks, and an `index.ts` that exports the three things other features are allowed to import — plus a review comment when someone deep-imports past it. Nest gives the backend that exact shape, and enforces it at boot instead of in review: if `RemindersModule` doesn't import `TasksModule`, and `TasksModule` doesn't list `TasksService` in `exports`, then injecting it fails when the process starts. Not at 2am under load. At startup, with a message naming the missing dependency.

Most of the vocabulary is a rename of what you already built by hand:

| What you wrote in Express | What Nest calls it |
|---|---|
| `routes/tasks.ts` | controller |
| `services/task.service.ts` | provider |
| the wiring in `app.ts` | module |
| `requireAuth` middleware | guard |
| `taskSchema.parse(req.body)` | pipe |
| error-handling middleware | exception filter |

## In practice

A module is a declaration of four lists: what it needs, what it serves over HTTP, what it can inject, and what it lets others inject.

```ts
// tasks/tasks.module.ts
@Module({
  imports: [SupabaseModule],        // dependencies I need
  controllers: [TasksController],   // HTTP surface I expose
  providers: [TasksService],        // injectables I own
  exports: [TasksService],          // my public API to other modules
})
export class TasksModule {}
```

The controller does exactly what your route file did, minus the plumbing. Decorators replace the router calls, and parameter decorators replace reaching into `req`:

```ts
@Controller("tasks")
export class TasksController {
  constructor(private readonly tasks: TasksService) {}

  @Get(":id")
  async findOne(@Param("id") id: string, @CurrentUser() user: AuthUser) {
    return this.tasks.findOne(user.id, id);
  }

  @Post()
  async create(@Body() dto: CreateTaskDto, @CurrentUser() user: AuthUser) {
    return this.tasks.create(user.id, dto);
  }
}
```

Note what is *absent*: no try/catch, no `res.status(...)`, no manual validation call. Those become filters and pipes, which is day 29.

The service is a plain class with a decorator and a constructor that asks for things:

```ts
@Injectable()
export class TasksService {
  constructor(private readonly db: SupabaseClientProvider) {}

  async findOne(userId: string, id: string) {
    const task = await this.db.forUser(userId).from("tasks").selectOne(id);
    if (!task) throw new NotFoundException("Task not found");
    return task;
  }
}
```

At boot, Nest walks that graph. `TasksController` needs `TasksService`; `TasksService` needs `SupabaseClientProvider`, which `SupabaseModule` exports. It constructs each one **once** and hands the same instance to everyone. If a link is missing you get the error you will see most often in your first week:

```text
Nest can't resolve dependencies of the TasksService (?).
Please make sure that the argument at index [0] is available in the TasksModule context.
```

Read the `?`. It marks which constructor argument the container couldn't satisfy — you either forgot to list the provider, or forgot to import (or export) the module that owns it.

How does Nest know the constructor wants a `SupabaseClientProvider` at runtime, when TypeScript types are erased? Because `emitDecoratorMetadata` in `tsconfig.json` makes the compiler write the parameter types into metadata, and `reflect-metadata` lets Nest read them back. That is the whole magic trick, and it's why a broken tsconfig produces bizarre injection failures.

Let the CLI write the boilerplate: `nest g resource tasks` scaffolds module, controller, service, DTOs, and a spec file, then wires the module into `app.module.ts` for you.

## In your world

Don't rewrite the whole Task Manager today. Take **one** slice — the tasks resource — into a fresh `nest new` app and port `GET /tasks`, `GET /tasks/:id`, and `POST /tasks`. Keep the same Supabase project, the same tables. What you're measuring is not "did it work" but the trade: more ceremony per endpoint (a controller, a service, a module, a DTO where you had one handler), and far less wiring per feature (a new resource touches its own folder and one line of `app.module.ts`, not a 300-line file).

Then open your NestJS backend at work and read `src/` like a map. You'll find a feature folder per resource, each with a controller, a service, DTOs, and a repository; a `common/` tree holding the cross-cutting machinery (auth, errors, response shaping, pagination, logging); a router layer that groups feature modules under URL prefixes and roles; a `configs/` folder; and `app.module.ts` at the root importing all of it. That project persists to MongoDB through Mongoose rather than Postgres — proof that none of this is database-specific. The structure is the point.

## Watch out

- **`new SomeService()` outside DI gives you a second instance** with its own state. Any in-memory cache, counter, or connection inside it silently diverges from the one the container manages, and nothing errors. This is why your own standards say services are injectable and never `new`ed.
- **Circular module imports** deadlock the container. `forwardRef()` unblocks you, but treat it as a smell: two modules that need each other usually want a third module holding the shared piece.
- **Providers are singletons by default.** Request-scoped providers exist, and they're contagious — anything that injects a request-scoped provider becomes request-scoped too, and gets constructed per request. Reach for it only for genuinely per-request state, like a database handle carrying the caller's identity.
- **The temptation to write logic in the controller** is strongest exactly when the logic is small. Two lines of "just check the owner here" is how controllers become untestable, because a controller's dependencies are HTTP-shaped and a service's are not.

