## The problem

A signup endpoint takes an email and a password. The DTO declares exactly those two fields. Someone reads the API docs, opens a REST client, and posts a third:

```json
{ "email": "me@example.com", "password": "hunter2", "role": "admin" }
```

The response is `201 Created`. The audit log shows a clean, successful signup. The new account can read every other user's orders. The DTO *was* validated — the validator confirmed email and password were present and well-formed, then handed the whole original body to the service, which passed it to the ORM, which happily wrote `role: "admin"`. That's **mass assignment**, and it is one config flag away from impossible.

Same week, different incident: a query throws and the 500 response body carries the full stack trace. An attacker now knows your absolute file paths, your ORM and its version, and the shape of the failing query. Your mobile app shows the user a raw `TypeError`, because there was no consistent error envelope for it to read.

## The idea

Both bugs are one mistake in two outfits: the boundary was treated as a suggestion instead of a filter. A boundary that only checks *what must be present* isn't one. It also has to decide what happens to everything else — strip it, or reject the request.

Nest's second contribution is **named slots** for boundary rules, so each rule lives in one place instead of being copy-pasted into forty handlers. There are five, in a fixed order:

```text
middleware → guard → interceptor (before) → pipe → handler
           → interceptor (after) → exception filter (if anything threw)
```

Picture a passenger moving through an airport. Middleware is the building — doors, signage, cameras — it sees traffic but knows nothing about your flight. The guard is the agent deciding whether you get past this point at all. The pipe is the scanner that inspects your bag and confiscates what isn't allowed. The interceptor wraps your whole journey, timing it and stamping the outcome. The filter is the desk you're sent to when something goes wrong, so every problem produces the same paperwork.

| Slot | What it can see | Reach for it when |
|---|---|---|
| Middleware | raw request and response, no handler context | helmet, CORS, body parsing, request ids, access logs |
| Guard | the execution context plus route metadata | "may this caller proceed?" — authentication, roles, ownership |
| Interceptor | before **and** after the handler; can wrap the result | response envelopes, timing, caching, timeouts |
| Pipe | one argument's value | validating and transforming DTOs, parsing params |
| Filter | a thrown exception | one consistent error shape for the whole API |

## In practice

A DTO is a class whose decorators describe the allowed shape:

```ts
export class CreateTaskDto {
  @IsString() @IsNotEmpty() @MaxLength(200)
  title: string;

  @IsOptional() @IsInt() @Min(1) @Max(3)
  priority?: number;
}
```

The flag that closes the hole is on the global pipe, in `main.ts`:

```ts
app.useGlobalPipes(
  new ValidationPipe({
    whitelist: true,            // silently strip keys no DTO field declares
    forbidNonWhitelisted: true, // or better: reject the request with a 400
    transform: true,            // "2" becomes 2, plain body becomes a DTO instance
  }),
);
```

`whitelist` makes `role: "admin"` disappear before your service sees it. `forbidNonWhitelisted` upgrades silence to a loud 400. `transform` is the `z.coerce` you've been writing, and it's also what turns the plain body into a real instance of the DTO class.

You've been doing this with Zod for four weeks. Both tools do the same job in the same place and differ in direction:

| | Zod | class-validator |
|---|---|---|
| Direction | schema first, type inferred from it | class first, decorators annotate it |
| Lives as | a value you can compose, extend, reuse | a type the DI container and Swagger can read |
| Strips unknown keys | `.strict()` / `.strip()` on the schema | `whitelist` on the pipe |
| Nest integration | works fine behind a small custom pipe | native: pipes, Swagger schemas, DI-aware validators |

Neither is better. Zod composes more elegantly; class-validator hands Nest one object it can use for validation, OpenAPI generation, and serialization at once. Use whichever the codebase already uses — the load-bearing word in both is **boundary**.

Responses need the same discipline in reverse. Never return a database entity from a controller:

```ts
export class TaskResponseDto {
  @Expose() id: string;
  @Expose() title: string;

  @Exclude() internalOwnerId: string;
  @Exclude() deletedAt: Date;
}
```

With `ClassSerializerInterceptor` registered globally, `@Exclude()` fields are dropped on the way out. The catch that bites everyone once: it only works on **class instances**. A plain object from your ORM carries no decorators, so nothing is excluded and the password hash ships. Return `plainToInstance(TaskResponseDto, row)`, and add a test asserting the secret field is absent.

Now authentication, where your own rule earns its keep. Register the guard globally and make public routes opt out:

```ts
// app.module.ts — global, and still able to inject dependencies
providers: [{ provide: APP_GUARD, useClass: JwtAuthGuard }]
```

```ts
@Injectable()
export class JwtAuthGuard extends AuthGuard("jwt") {
  constructor(private reflector: Reflector) { super(); }

  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext) {
    const isPublic = this.reflector.getAllAndOverride(IS_PUBLIC_KEY, [
      context.getHandler(),
      context.getClass(),
    ]);
    return isPublic ? true : super.canActivate(context);
  }
}
```

`@Public()` is just `SetMetadata(IS_PUBLIC_KEY, true)`. The direction matters more than the code: with a global guard, forgetting a decorator leaves a route **locked** and someone files a bug. With per-route `@UseGuards(...)`, forgetting it leaves the route **open** and nobody files anything. Default-closed makes your mistakes noisy.

Errors get one shape, decided in one file:

```ts
@Catch()
export class AllExceptionsFilter implements ExceptionFilter {
  catch(err: unknown, host: ArgumentsHost) {
    const res = host.switchToHttp().getResponse();
    const status = err instanceof HttpException ? err.getStatus() : 500;
    res.status(status).json({
      statusCode: status,
      message: err instanceof HttpException ? err.getResponse() : "Internal server error",
      requestId: res.locals.requestId,
    });
  }
}
```

Throw typed exceptions from services — `NotFoundException`, `UnauthorizedException`, `ConflictException` — and the filter maps them to status codes. Anything untyped becomes a generic 500, with the detail logged server-side and never serialized. That's incident number two closed.

Config gets the day-5 fail-fast treatment, except the schema runs before the HTTP server binds:

```ts
ConfigModule.forRoot({
  isGlobal: true,
  validationSchema: Joi.object({
    NODE_ENV: Joi.string().valid("development", "staging", "production").required(),
    DATABASE_URL: Joi.string().uri().required(),
    JWT_SECRET: Joi.string().min(32).required(),
  }),
});
```

A missing secret should kill the process at boot, not produce a 500 at 3am on the one endpoint that reads it.

Testing is where DI pays you back. A unit test builds a module holding only what it needs:

```ts
const moduleRef = await Test.createTestingModule({
  controllers: [TasksController],
  providers: [{ provide: TasksService, useValue: { findOne: jest.fn() } }],
}).compile();
```

No database, no network, milliseconds. For end-to-end, boot the real app and drive it over HTTP with supertest:

```ts
const app = moduleRef.createNestApplication();
app.useGlobalPipes(new ValidationPipe({ whitelist: true }));
await app.init();
await request(app.getHttpServer()).post("/tasks").send({ title: "x", role: "admin" }).expect(400);
```

That last line is the regression test for the incident at the top of this module. Write it once and mass assignment can't come back.

## In your world

You own two backends built on opposite philosophies, and both choices are correct. The social app's backend is Bun and Hono: small surface, edge-friendly, every millisecond and dependency visible. The other is NestJS: many contributors, years of lifespan, structure the framework won't let anyone skip.

| Question to ask | Points to NestJS | Points to Hono / Express |
|---|---|---|
| How many people touch this per quarter? | five or more, rotating | one to three, stable |
| How long will it live? | years, dozens of features | months, or a focused service |
| Where does it run? | a Node container | edge, workers, Bun, cold-start sensitive |
| Who enforces structure? | the framework, at boot | code review and discipline |
| Cost of one new endpoint | four files and some ceremony | one handler |
| Cross-cutting concerns | first-class slots, applied globally | hand-rolled middleware you compose |
| What you pay | boot-time DI, heavier deps, a learning curve | vigilance, and eventual drift |

The choice is about team size and lifespan, not fashion. Now audit your NestJS project against this module: find where the global `ValidationPipe` is configured and whether `whitelist` is set; find how routes are protected and decide whether the default is open or closed; find the exception filter and confirm no stack trace can reach a response body in production. Write down what you find. These are the rules already in your own engineering standards — you'll be enforcing them in review soon, and that lands very differently when you can say *why*.

## Watch out

- **Guard order and scope.** Globals run before route-scoped guards, and a guard registered with `useGlobalGuards()` cannot inject anything — use the `APP_GUARD` provider token when your guard needs a `Reflector` or a service.
- **No `whitelist: true` means mass assignment is still wide open,** even with a perfectly decorated DTO. Validation that only checks required fields is not a boundary.
- **Returning entities directly** leaks whatever the table happens to hold today — password hashes, soft-delete flags, internal ids, that column someone added last sprint. Response DTOs are the only reliable answer.
- **`@Public()` at the wrong level.** On the controller, it unlocks every route in it, including the ones you add next month. Put it on the single handler that needs it.
- **Interceptors that swallow errors.** A `catchError` that returns an empty value turns failures into `200 OK` with a null body. Your monitoring goes quiet, your dashboards look healthy, and the mobile app renders an empty screen.
